<div class="vs-hero">
  <h1>Hair Transplant: China vs Turkey (2026)</h1>
  <p>Turkey — Istanbul especially — is the world's hair-transplant capital. China is the rising value alternative. This is an honest, data-driven comparison so you can choose the right one for your case and budget.</p>
</div>

<div class="vs-card">
  <h2>💈 Cost Comparison: FUE Hair Transplant (per 3,000 grafts)</h2>
  <table class="vs-table">
    <thead><tr><th>Destination</th><th>Per Graft</th><th>3,000 Grafts</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>🇨🇳 China</strong></td><td class="win">$0.80–$2.00</td><td class="win">$2,400–$5,400</td><td>DHI technique $1.50–$3.00/graft</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>🇹🇷 Turkey</strong></td><td>$1.20–$2.50</td><td>$3,600–$7,500</td><td>Often bundled with hotel + transfer</td></tr>
      <tr><td>🇺🇸 United States</td><td>$5–$8</td><td>$15,000–$24,000</td><td>Reference point</td></tr>
      <tr><td>🇰🇷 South Korea</td><td>$6–$9</td><td>$18,000–$27,000</td><td>Reference point</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note">Prices are 2026 estimates for FUE, excluding travel and accommodation. Turkey package deals often fold the hotel into the quote, so compare total trip cost, not just per-graft price.</p>
</div>

<div class="vs-card">
  <h2>🏥 Quality, Volume &amp; Experience</h2>
  <p><strong>Turkey</strong> performs an estimated <strong>500,000+ hair transplants a year</strong> and has thousands of dedicated clinics, many serving almost exclusively international patients. The all-inclusive model — airport pickup, hotel, procedure, follow-up — is refined. The flip side: quality varies widely between clinics, and "hair mill" operations that push volume over artistry are a real risk you must vet against.</p>
  <p><strong>China's</strong> top hair-transplant chains use the same FUE/DHI equipment (WAW FUE systems, Choi implanter pens) as clinics in Turkey and Korea, and their surgeons perform hundreds of procedures monthly. The advantage is hospital-based oversight at accredited facilities and a lower price. The trade-off: China's hair-transplant <em>tourism</em> brand is younger, and English-speaking dedicated clinics are fewer (usually handled through an international department or coordinator).</p>
</div>

<div class="vs-card">
  <h2>🎯 Which Should You Choose?</h2>
  <div class="verdict">
    <div class="box china">
      <h4>Choose China if…</h4>
      <ul>
        <li>Budget is the top priority — lowest per-graft price</li>
        <li>You want hospital-based oversight (JCI / Grade 3A)</li>
        <li>You're combining a hair transplant with another procedure or check-up in one trip</li>
        <li>You're coming from Asia — shorter flight, no long-haul cost</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="box other">
      <h4>Choose Turkey if…</h4>
      <ul>
        <li>You want the world's most established hair-transplant brand</li>
        <li>An all-inclusive package (hotel + transfer + procedure) matters</li>
        <li>You prefer a clinic built entirely around international patients</li>
        <li>Maximum clinic choice and English-first service are priorities</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>Neither is "better" in the abstract — Turkey leads on volume and brand, China leads on price and hospital oversight. The honest answer depends on whether you optimize for the proven tourism experience or the lowest total cost with accredited oversight.</p>
</div>

<div class="vs-card">
  <h2>🛂 Visa &amp; Logistics</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Turkey:</strong> e-Visa or visa-free for most nationalities; 3–4 hour flight from Europe.</li>
    <li><strong>China:</strong> an L (tourist/medical) visa is standard, and some nationalities qualify for 240-hour transit visa-free. See the <a href="/visa-guide.html" style="color:#1e3c72;font-weight:600">visa guide</a> for every option.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

